With the latest macOS 27.3 beta, Apple is giving Mac users more control over the data they share and the access they give to apps, including apps that use AI agents. Apple today informed developers about upcoming changes to the Full Disk Access API and the Device Control and Data Access API.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/claude-warning.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1077759" /><br/>
Here's what <a href="https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=oqng0zjk">Apple says is changing</a>:<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li>The current Device Control and Data Access setting will be split into two options that make it clearer to users what they're permitting. The setting is becoming System &amp; App Protection Override and Accessibility.</li><br/>
 	<li>There will be a single method for granting permissions by selecting the app requesting access from a list.</li><br/>
 	<li>Apple will encourage users to grant access to just the specific apps (in System &amp; App Protection Override) or folders (for Full Disk Access) required for workflows.</li><br/>
 	<li>Apple will better inform users about the data apps will receive access to or control over if full permissions are given.</li><br/>
 	<li>Users will have an option to configure apps to ask each time for required access rather than granting an app unrestricted access.</li><br/>
 	<li>There will be a transparency indicator when a requesting app is controlling another app with System &amp; App Protection Override. The indicator will be displayed in the Mac menu bar.</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
Apple says developers will need to adjust user onboarding and make sure apps can "gracefully handle" access to a limited set of folders or apps when using the APIs being overhauled.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/claude-warning-2-2.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1077765" /><br/>
Apps that use Full Disk Access for disk backup or the new Accessibility settings for assistive technologies can request entitlements to offer the same experience for new installations that they offered before macOS 27.3.<br/>
<br/>
Right now, known apps keep their current experience without an entitlement, and app permissions granted by users prior to macOS 27.3 will remain in effect. Mac users will see these changes when approving new app permission requests, and entitlement details are coming soon.<br/>
<br/>
